1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,278
Take a double cone, seen here from the side, and slice it with a flat plane, the yellow line.

2
00:00:06,278 --> 00:00:08,027
A level cut gives a circle.

3
00:00:08,233 --> 00:00:11,151
Tilt the plane a little: an ellipse.

4
00:00:11,151 --> 00:00:18,013
Tilt it until it runs parallel to the side of the cone: the ellipse breaks open into a parabola.

5
00:00:18,013 --> 00:00:24,157
Tilt it further, so it cuts both halves of the cone: a hyperbola, in two branches.

6
00:00:24,367 --> 00:00:27,910
Four curves that look nothing alike, from one construction.

7
00:00:27,910 --> 00:00:34,069
They are the paths of planets and comets, the shape of a satellite dish and a cooling tower.

8
00:00:34,069 --> 00:00:38,529
And, as you will see, they all obey one single rule.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,456
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 8: Conic Sections.

10
00:00:50,833 --> 00:00:51,941
Six parts.

11
00:00:51,941 --> 00:00:54,832
One definition for all four curves.

12
00:00:54,832 --> 00:00:56,941
When a line touches a circle.

13
00:00:56,941 --> 00:00:59,676
Tangents and normals to a circle.

14
00:00:59,676 --> 00:01:07,285
Then the parabola, the ellipse and the hyperbola, each with its standard equation, its elements and its tangents.

15
00:01:11,633 --> 00:01:13,484
Here is the single rule.

16
00:01:13,484 --> 00:01:18,327
Fix a point S, the focus, and a line, the directrix.

17
00:01:18,327 --> 00:01:26,791
A conic is the path of a point P whose distance from the focus is always a fixed multiple e of its distance from the directrix.

18
00:01:26,791 --> 00:01:28,893
The number e is the eccentricity.

19
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:30,404
Watch P go round.

20
00:01:30,404 --> 00:01:34,974
Its two distances change, but their ratio stays at point six.

21
00:01:34,974 --> 00:01:36,263
That is an ellipse.

22
00:01:36,263 --> 00:01:45,201
Make e exactly one and you get a parabola; more than one, a hyperbola; and as e shrinks to zero, a circle.

23
00:01:46,433 --> 00:01:50,119
You can name a conic from its equation before doing any algebra.

24
00:01:50,119 --> 00:01:52,229
Look only at the squared terms.

25
00:01:52,229 --> 00:01:54,729
Equal coefficients: a circle.

26
00:01:54,729 --> 00:01:57,099
One square missing: a parabola.

27
00:01:57,099 --> 00:02:00,575
Unequal but the same sign: an ellipse.

28
00:02:00,575 --> 00:02:03,016
Opposite signs: a hyperbola.

29
00:02:03,233 --> 00:02:14,888
So four x squared plus nine y squared is an ellipse; nine x squared minus sixteen y squared, a hyperbola; and an equation with only y squared, a parabola.

30
00:02:14,888 --> 00:02:17,519
Name it first, then complete the square.

31
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:28,028
A circle is every point at distance r from a centre h, k.

32
00:02:28,028 --> 00:02:31,538
The distance formula, squared, gives the standard form.

33
00:02:31,767 --> 00:02:36,204
With the centre at the origin, x squared plus y squared equals r squared.

34
00:02:36,433 --> 00:02:45,849
Expand, and every circle looks like x squared plus y squared plus two g x plus two f y plus c equals zero.

35
00:02:45,849 --> 00:02:52,476
The centre is minus g, minus f: half the coefficients, signs changed.

36
00:02:52,476 --> 00:02:57,017
The radius is root of g squared plus f squared minus c.

37
00:02:57,233 --> 00:03:03,594
And if you know the ends of a diameter, the angle in a semicircle is a right angle, which gives the diameter form.

38
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:08,437
A trap: if the squares carry a coefficient, divide it out first.

39
00:03:08,437 --> 00:03:16,189
Three x squared plus three y squared and so on: divide by three, and the centre is two, minus three.

40
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:21,777
Slide a line of fixed slope across a circle of radius two.

41
00:03:21,777 --> 00:03:24,615
Watch the red distance from the centre to the line.

42
00:03:24,615 --> 00:03:28,293
Less than the radius: the line cuts the circle twice.

43
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:32,486
Equal to the radius: it just touches, a tangent.

44
00:03:32,486 --> 00:03:35,086
More than the radius: it misses altogether.

45
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:47,359
So y equals m x plus c touches x squared plus y squared equals a squared exactly when the distance, c over root of one plus m squared, equals a.

46
00:03:47,359 --> 00:03:51,751
That is, c squared equals a squared times one plus m squared.

47
00:03:52,967 --> 00:03:53,711
Three points.

48
00:03:54,200 --> 00:04:02,292
Put each into the general form: c is zero, g is minus three, f is minus four.

49
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:08,531
So the circle is x squared plus y squared minus six x minus eight y equals zero.

50
00:04:08,733 --> 00:04:11,649
Centre three, four; radius five.

51
00:04:13,667 --> 00:04:16,378
Touching means distance equals radius.

52
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:22,077
The perpendicular distance from three, four to the line is ten over five: two.

53
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:27,186
So the circle is x minus three squared plus y minus four squared equals four.

54
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:30,033
Use the condition.

55
00:04:30,433 --> 00:04:35,839
c squared is twenty times five, a hundred: c is plus or minus ten.

56
00:04:36,067 --> 00:04:43,230
The point of contact is minus a squared m over c, a squared over c: minus four, two.

57
00:04:43,230 --> 00:04:45,673
It lies on both the line and the circle.

58
00:04:51,267 --> 00:04:56,256
At any point of a circle, the tangent is perpendicular to the radius.

59
00:04:56,256 --> 00:04:58,858
Move the point round and the right angle stays.

60
00:04:59,067 --> 00:05:07,363
The radius to x one, y one has slope y one over x one, so the tangent has slope minus x one over y one.

61
00:05:07,363 --> 00:05:15,813
Simplify, using the fact that the point is on the circle, and the tangent is x x one plus y y one equals a squared.

62
00:05:15,813 --> 00:05:27,428
For a general circle, replace x squared by x x one, y squared by y y one, two x by x plus x one, and two y by y plus y one.

63
00:05:27,428 --> 00:05:29,970
And the normal always passes through the centre.

64
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:32,476
Straight substitution.

65
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:34,855
First check that the point is on the circle.

66
00:05:35,067 --> 00:05:38,658
Tangent: three x plus four y equals twenty five.

67
00:05:38,867 --> 00:05:45,561
Normal: the line through the centre and the point: four x minus three y equals zero.

68
00:05:45,561 --> 00:05:47,514
The slopes multiply to minus one.

69
00:05:49,533 --> 00:05:55,563
From the outside point seven, one, draw the two tangents to the circle of radius five.

70
00:05:55,563 --> 00:06:02,938
The tangent length is root of S one: root of forty nine plus one minus twenty five, which is five.

71
00:06:03,167 --> 00:06:10,890
For the tangents themselves, take any line through seven, one with slope m and apply the tangency condition.

72
00:06:10,890 --> 00:06:15,351
The quadratic gives m equals four thirds or minus three quarters.

73
00:06:15,567 --> 00:06:20,530
Their slopes multiply to minus one: the tangents are perpendicular.

74
00:06:20,530 --> 00:06:21,884
That is no accident.

75
00:06:21,884 --> 00:06:32,724
Seven squared plus one squared is fifty, twice twenty five, so the point lies on the director circle, the circle from which every pair of tangents meets at a right angle.

76
00:06:34,533 --> 00:06:35,902
Exam tip.

77
00:06:35,902 --> 00:06:46,084
If the line is in the form y equals m x plus c and the circle is centred at the origin, use c squared equals a squared, one plus m squared.

78
00:06:46,084 --> 00:06:49,961
Otherwise, use distance from the centre equals the radius.

